<section class="homecam-section homecam-muted" id="storage-calculation"><div class="container"><h2 class="section-title">A transparent <span>storage estimate.</span></h2><p>For continuous recording, a decimal-capacity estimate is: <strong>GB per day = total recorded Mbps × 10.8</strong>. Multiply by required days, then allow for audio, filesystem overhead, bitrate variation and a practical margin. Read bitrate values from the actual recording streams; resolution alone is insufficient.</p><div class="homecam-note"><strong>Worked example, not a package promise</strong><p>Eight cameras at an assumed 2Mbps each total 16Mbps: approximately 172.8GB per day or 2,419.2GB over 14 days, before overhead and margin. A nominal 2TB drive therefore would not meet this example's 14-day target. Real variable-bitrate cameras can consume more or less depending on activity and detail.</p></div><p>Motion-only recording needs measured activity rather than an invented percentage saving. Lowering bitrate or frame rate can extend retention while reducing useful detail. We check playback quality at the relevant view before accepting that trade-off.</p><a class="homecam-link" href="<?= SITE_URL ?>/ip-camera-installation-karachi">Understand IP camera streams and recording paths</a></div></section>
<section class="homecam-section" id="storage-upgrade"><div class="container"><h2 class="section-title">Protect footage <span>before changing a disk.</span></h2><ol class="homecam-handover"><li><strong>Agree preservation:</strong> identify incident time windows and channels; export authorised footage and check it plays on separate equipment.</li><li><strong>Check compatibility:</strong> confirm the recorder's drive limits and suitable workload-rated storage. If replacing the recorder, also check camera protocols, incoming bandwidth and stream resolution.</li><li><strong>Plan downtime:</strong> agree when recording can stop. Shut down according to the manufacturer procedure and disconnect power before internal work.</li><li><strong>Install and configure:</strong> identify the new disk correctly before initialisation. Set schedules, overwrite policy and exception alerts; record any changes to image quality.</li><li><strong>Handle the old disk:</strong> label it and agree return, retention or secure disposal with the owner. Do not assume formatting is secure erasure.</li></ol><p>We do not promise recovery of overwritten or failed-disk footage. If evidence is missing, stop destructive storage work and agree whether specialist recovery assessment is appropriate.</p></div></section>
<section class="homecam-section homecam-muted" id="storage-handover"><div class="container"><h2 class="section-title">Verify recording <span>after the upgrade.</span></h2><p>At handover, check every channel records, the clock and time zone are correct, a recent clip plays and export works. Confirm disk status and supported alerts. Test local recording separately from internet-dependent remote viewing.</p><p>A fresh disk cannot demonstrate 14 or 30 days of retention on installation day. Record the estimate and arrange a later retention check after enough time has elapsed. Compare oldest playable footage by channel with the target and investigate unexplained gaps.</p><p>The handover record should list recorder and disk models, installed capacity, stream settings, schedules, footage exports, removed-disk handling and unresolved limits. Keep owner accounts and recovery details under customer control.</p><p><a href="<?= SITE_URL ?>/cctv-maintenance-karachi">Diagnose missing playback or disk warnings</a> · <a href="<?= SITE_URL ?>/shop-cctv-installation-karachi">Plan retail incident coverage</a> · <a href="<?= SITE_URL ?>/home-cctv-installation-karachi">Plan home camera views</a></p></div></section>
